2180f5d6-c677-4f63-b929-129da5187cab	[CF 1600] Fullmetal Bitchemist	cf	cf-2237-d	https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/2237/D	Hard	1600	### Problem Statement\n\nA binary string is a string consisting only of characters $0$ and $1$. The two characters $0$ and $1$ are called opposite values.\n\nConsider a binary string $t$. Let $|t|$ be the length of $t$. When $|t| \\ge 2$, for every $1 \\le i  \\lt  |t|$, the characters $t_i$ and $t_{i+1}$ are adjacent.\n\nA binary string $t$ is called *beautiful* if it can be reduced to a string of length exactly $1$ by applying the following operation any number of times, possibly zero:\n\n -  Choose two equal adjacent characters, remove both of them, and insert one character with the opposite value in their place. \nFor example, the string $\\mathtt{10001}$ can become $\\mathtt{1101}$ by replacing the first adjacent pair $\\mathtt{00}$ with $\\mathtt{1}$. Then it can become $\\mathtt{001}$, then $\\mathtt{11}$, and finally $\\mathtt{0}$. Therefore, $\\mathtt{10001}$ is beautiful. \n\nOn the other hand, $\\mathtt{111}$ is not beautiful. After one operation, it becomes $\\mathtt{01}$, and then no operation can be applied.\n\nYou are given a binary string $s$. Compute the number of non-empty beautiful substrings$^{\\text{∗}}$ of $s$.\n\n$^{\\text{∗}}$A string $a$ is a substring of a string $b$ if $a$ can be obtained from $b$ by the deletion of several (possibly, zero or all) characters from the beginning and several (possibly, zero or all) characters from the end.\n\n### Input\nEach test contains multiple test cases. The first line contains the number of test cases $t$ ($1 \\le t \\le 10^4$). The description of the test cases follows.\n\nThe first line of each test case contains an integer $n$ ($1 \\le n \\le 10^6$) — the length of $s$.\n\nThe second line of each test case contains a binary string $s$ of length $n$.\n\nIt is guaranteed that the sum of $n$ over all test cases does not exceed $10^6$.\n\n### Output\nFor each test case, output a single integer — the number of beautiful substrings of $s$.\n\n### Note\nIn the first test case, the only non-empty substring is $\\mathtt{0}$, which is already a binary string of length $1$. Therefore, it is beautiful.\n\nIn the second test case, the beautiful substrings are $\\mathtt{0}$ and $\\mathtt{1}$. The substring $\\mathtt{01}$ is not beautiful, because its two characters are not equal, so no operation can be applied.\n\nIn the third test case, the beautiful substrings are: \n\n -  $s[1,1]=\\mathtt{0}$; \n-  $s[2,2]=\\mathtt{1}$; \n-  $s[3,3]=\\mathtt{0}$; \n-  $s[4,4]=\\mathtt{0}$; \n-  $s[5,5]=\\mathtt{1}$; \n-  $s[3,4]=\\mathtt{00}$, which can become $\\mathtt{1}$; \n-  $s[2,4]=\\mathtt{100}$, which can become $\\mathtt{11}$, then $\\mathtt{0}$; \n-  $s[3,5]=\\mathtt{001}$, which can become $\\mathtt{11}$, then $\\mathtt{0}$; \n-  $s[1,4]=\\mathtt{0100}$, which can become $\\mathtt{011}$, then $\\mathtt{00}$, then $\\mathtt{1}$; \n-  $s[1,5]=\\mathtt{01001}$, which can become $\\mathtt{0111}$, then $\\mathtt{001}$, then $\\mathtt{11}$, and finally $\\mathtt{0}$. \nThus, there are $10$ beautiful substrings.	[{"input": "10\\n1\\n0\\n2\\n01\\n5\\n01001\\n3\\n001\\n6\\n011110\\n9\\n010110110\\n12\\n010000101001\\n16\\n1010011010010110\\n20\\n11110101101101001110\\n30\\n000101100011111001111100000010", "output": "1\\n2\\n10\\n5\\n15\\n30\\n47\\n81\\n139\\n316"}]	2026-10-29 16:33:18.026	2026-10-29 15:49:10.738	2026-10-29 16:33:18.028	256	2000
